So not after about... level 9 or so?  Again, don't know about you, but
the poison instakills are well recognized to be extremely annoying by
everyone but the rawest newbies, and so everyone rushes for poison
resistance out of the gate.

The point I'm trying to make here is that an idea like this can both be 
a good idea on its own and, simultaneously, not be likely to show up in any
sort of time to matter, rendering it into just another "Hey, that thing.
Yeah!  I found one of those on DL2 once, it was nice for awhile and then
I wished up speed boots off a lamp" -- see "boots of kicking."  And perhaps 
this is all that this idea should end up being, but while my personal
litmus test for "too strong" is "will people wish for this ahead of
SDSM, the Eye, Magicbane, etc?", I also would like to at least make the
items worth wanting no matter when you find them.

This is of course part of the problem with vanilla at the moment; because 
the player can be a walking tank by the time they reach the Castle, huge
portions of item-generation land (Gehennom) become irrelevant.  More
below where you explicitly mention the topic.

>> >> > Boots of Running
>> I thought about bolt-on hacks for speed a couple months ago, and then
>> started digging into a way to just redo the speed system and split
>> "movement" and "attack" speeds up somehow.  There are some problems with
>> making monsters faster than speed 12 ... (etc)
> Is it possible to give the critter an extra chance through just the
> movement routine if he's just moved?

The critter's movement routine _is_ its attack routine.  It's certainly
possible to pry that out, but after a point you get to where you're
rewriting the speed system anyway and start asking yourself questions
like "why not just do it right?"

>> > A successful jump can consistantly carry the player three spaces,
>> > right?  Speed boots can't match that for fleeing.
>> Unless you have to turn a corner...
> That's not an issue in zig-zag corridors (they can be moved through
> diagonally), 

As they can for speed boots, too.  I'm more thinking of something like
Gehennom's mazes, especially in circumstances where your light doesn't
carry that knight's move away that you'd like.  In open space, jumping
boots are faster; in confined spaces, the three squares may not be
available to you.

On Astral, jumping works so well because the choke points are only choke
points; there are wide open spaces/rooms behind each, and the player is
actively using teleportation wands to clear a path for himself to jump.
(Many players are also using the Candelabrum to get a huge light radius
for the Wizard's extra space on straight jumps.)

> The reason I brought it up is a more profound issue than just finding
> specific items.  Nethack now easily contains over 70 dungeon levels in
> a typical winning game.  Given normal treasure generation, guarenteed
> items and death drops, the player will typically find nearly all the
> things the game can generate on a single trip.  

The problem with assuming all 70 levels is that the Castle appears
between 25 and 29 (ish?).  At that point, the player obtains the wand,
and has no more need for most items except for a small subset of the
expendables -- full healing, gain level, a few scroll types, etc.

Gehennom's great item hoards largely go unlooted because the player just
doesn't need the stuff anymore -- thus, who cares if you find boots of
foo on DL43?  You're already wearing your +4/+5 boots of Ascension Kit,
and you're not about to waste time swapping out because you're bored to
tears.  In vanilla this is exacerbated by the tedium required to explore
every level -- why bother slogging it all out, when you've already got 
more than enough to win?

So what it comes down to is that if you haven't found an item by DL25 or
so, it has to either be good enough to wish for, or happen to be
generated in the player's path at +2/+3 from that point forward; otherwise 
it ends up being just token flavor.  

> As Nethack's size has increased substantially the number items that 
> can be found in the dungeon has not.  Sokoban already generates one 
> random ring and wand per level, and tons of food.  One way to combat 
> this is to add more stuff.

While I see where you're going with this (make the types of items
generated more random, so you won't necessarily find speed boots in the
dungeon), I'm not sure it's necessarily solving the right problem; see
above.

>> > And aren't the really good boot types sometimes generated cursed?  
>> I'll wear cursed speed or jumping gladly unless it's like -4 as well.
>> Then I'll just wait and cancel them.
> But my point was, if you dump cursed boots because they might be
> fumble, you might dump a good type.

In vanilla, I don't dump them; I throw them into the first shop I come
across and price-ID them.  If they're base 30, they're lev or fumble, 
and I can come back for them later.  If not, they're speed, walking, or
jumping, and I fetch them out and put them on.

Assuming that these new boots would all be base 50 (my boots of molasses
were for _exactly_ this reason), I would just carry them along for
awhile, or toss 'em in the stash.  More about this below, though...

>> Yeah, but one of the first steps towards "skilled" a player takes is
>> "stop putting on unBUC'ed stuff".  Changes should be meaningful across
>> all facets of the game, not just "another evil thing that almost never
>> occurs except to newer players" -- see also "scroll of punishment".
> I'd argue that the change here should be towards forcing the player to
> take more BUC risks, by removing, or at least toning down, sources of
> non-scroll identification.

I agree.  Sporkhack did this in part with the racial/racist shopkeepers;
it adds one too many wrinkles to the realm of price-ID and takes it
beyond "casual memory".  It's still possible to spot the scroll of
identify easily, and this is as it should be -- have to have a bootstrap
point.  But most of my players who were experienced price-IDers (ie.
never required a blessed scroll of identify for anything more than
convenience) have simply reverted to the blessed ID and/or other
methods.  (The ones who weren't using price-ID, well, continue to not
use price-ID and don't notice much of a difference unless they're an elf 
trying to buy from an orc.  :)

It's not yet impossible to extract the formula from the source and
create an "automatic" version of it; if I ever think that's actually a 
problem, I have a fix in hand to solve that as well.  I don't currently 
think that's a problem, though.

The biggest problem with being too aggressive about that is that forcing
the player to save ID scrolls and use the blessed scroll of ID for
everything is _also_ somewhat tedious.  I'm looking at ways right now to
provide more "remove curse" functionality; say, perhaps, priests offering 
it as a service (uncurse all worn/wielded items) for a nice hunk of gold?

When it's not a pain in the butt to get cursed things off, it's far more
likely that players will try possibly-cursed things on.

