>> > Anyway, a possibly-cursed item cannot make the game too annoying to
>> > play, and they have to make the player move randomly less often than
>> > confusion, which is already not that bad in generic situations.
>> Er, right.  It's not annoying at all, is my point.  If you make it
>> annoying, it just goes onto the list of "why you don't try on random
>> boots" along with fumbling.
> Then I ask you, what could cause the player to take that risk?

Having the reward be enough to offset the risk.

For an item like Boots of Evilfoo, you can't directly increase the
reward, and the other types of boots are already a fixed reward.  So you
have to either decrease the risk for putting the boots on (provide more 
sources of remove curse), or increase the attendant risk of NOT having 
some kind of good boots (making the overall game harder such that speed 
boots, jumping boots, etc. are "better put these on, they might be speed" 
as opposed to just "oh, cool, magic boots, i'll save them to ID later").

